', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'degrees of excellence the imamate pole of religion', 'the imamate pole of religion the treatise opens and al naysaburi begins with a claim most believers would find backwards the true pole of religion is not the messenger who brings the law but the imam who guards it and you cannot affirm the one without first affirming the other he argues that the world is never for a moment without an imam while the messenger comes and goes that god himself called the imam a messenger and the messenger an imam and that the necessity of the imamate is written into intellect and human nature as surely as the necessity of the creator then having conceded that people dispute the imam exactly as they disputed god he lays down the hinge of the whole book that reality gives two witnesses to the truth one established by sacred law and one compelled by nature the horizons and the souls themselves and that when both testify and the intellect weighs them without bias the truth of the imamate becomes impossible to miss', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'degrees of excellence the peak of every kind', 'the peak of every kind episode 1 sketched the ladder of degrees of excellence as an abstract engine here al naysaburi builds it himself rung by rung out of the raw materials of the world fire is the summit of the elements the sun of the planets gold of the metals and finer still the red ruby that comes through fire and water unchanged is the crown of all jewels wheat is the peak of the grains and the date palm of the trees so like a human being that it has male and female and must be fertilized each choice quietly names a virtue of the imam the one who gives light who cannot be corrupted who nourishes and heals and every bright image has its dark twin the healing antidote against the fatal poison the sweet tree against the thorned the fragrant plant against the putrid by which the usurpers of the imamate are shown for what they are the climb ends where it always pointed humankind is the summit of all creation and the imam is the summit of humankind without whom al naysaburi warns a person sinks beneath the beasts', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'degrees of excellence the imams authority', 'the imams authority the ladder has reached its summit now al naysaburi asks what the summit authorizes and his answers are among the boldest in the book all the worlds he argues were made for humanity and humanity for the imams so that whatever anyone takes without the imams leave is a kind of theft and all benefit is in the end a return to its source he grounds this in a ladder of spirits as the plant outranks the earth by the spirit of growth and the animal outranks the plant by sensation and the human outranks the animal by reason so the imam is given further spirits the sanctified and the creative that widen his dominion over all humankind from there he presses two more arguments that the imam is absolutely wise so that to charge him with a foolish act is to fall out of the faith and that reason alone without a line of scripture shows the world must have one supreme leader who is the best most knowing and most pure of all the shepherd without whom the flock is lost the head without which the body is a corpse', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'degrees of excellence worship and law without the imam', 'worship and law without the imam having proved the imams authority from nature and reason al naysaburi now finds it hidden inside the everyday obligations of islam human beings he argues are born knowing nothing and must be taught in a chain that ends in one taught by god alone so the imam is gods standing proof over creation and the perfect circle the number one and the indivisible point all image his uniqueness then he turns to the rites the qibla you face and the leader you follow in prayer share the very root of the word imam and the congregation multiplies your reward because it is performed behind him the pilgrimage begins with an intention aimed at him and the kaba in its barren valley teaches that he is reached only through hardship alms may be collected only by the imam holy war waged only under his banner and the prescribed penalties carried out only by his authority so that the leaders who claim the office trusted by their followers with blood and property but not with alms and bound by their own rule that a sinner cannot judge or testify refute themselves what remains al naysaburi concludes is that the true imamate belongs to the purified progeny of the prophets house', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'degrees of excellence prophets symbols and the caliphs', 'prophets symbols and the caliphs al naysaburi turns from doctrine to history and finds the imamate written across it if even a world holding none but sinless angels was given a vicegerent over the angels own objection then no world of flawed human beings can go without one and the imamate can never be settled by choice had it been the choice would have belonged to the angels noahs ark was the imam and noah named his heir by designation and each later prophets direction of prayer marks where the living guide stands moses toward jerusalem jesus toward the east and muhammad turning the people back to the kaba that abraham and ishmael built a sign that the imamate had returned to the house of abraham through ishmaels line then al naysaburi measures the first caliphs against the three virtues god honors knowledge holy struggle and piety and finds them wanting on the record of their own words the flight at hunayn abu bakrs confession that he was not the best among them and umars admission that the first oath had been a hasty slip', '');
', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'degrees of excellence the imam who mirrors god', 'the imam who mirrors god the treatise ends where its ladder always pointed every ordered domain has its peak so the imams too must have one and that utmost degree al naysaburi says is the commander of the faithful ali b abi talib the summit of all summits the perfector who gathers into himself the merits of every prophet and imam before him as the heart gives life to the limbs and the reigning imam of each age continues and completes that summit for his own time then comes the books most demanding claim that the imams dealing with people mirrors gods own as god gives to the pious and the impious alike conceals the means of worldly gain so no single path corrupts the world and moves between nearness and distance to keep his servants between fear and hope so does the imam his gifts unlike any monarchs seeking no return at all between those who reduce him to an ordinary ruler and those who exaggerate him into a god al naysaburi sets the true measure the universal vicegerent of god a shadow that imitates in every detail the one who casts it the series closes on the authors own prayer of gratitude and striving', '');
